\title{Augmented transport · shapes, labels and vertical extraction}
\taxon{Development}
\meta{layer}{human}
\meta{source}{Kernel/Augmented/Substitution.lean}
\tag{foundation}
\tag{augmented}

\p{This implements the API revision requested during review of the dependent-equality work. It refines the provisional presentation in \ref{dec-kr-0018} under \ref{at-fd-0007}, without changing the gate statement or its version. The equations remain those of Koudenburg, arXiv:1910.11189v4, Definition 1.2.}

\subtree{
  \title{Separate incidence from cell labels}
  \p{\code{Kernel.Augmented.RowShape} records the incident row boundaries and complete shared arrows. \code{RowShape.Labels} supplies one cell at each boundary. \code{rowEquiv} proves both split/join round trips, with cell count and horizontal input/output preserved. \code{CellGraph.Row.input} and \code{output} now compute through the shape. This is a lossless presentation of existing rows, not a canonical arity or general pasting theorem; \ref{dec-tl-0021} remains in force.}
  \p{\code{SubstitutionShape} contains all substitution incidence before labels are supplied. Its \code{outer} and \code{result} boundaries are independent of the cell graph. \code{Operations.apply} accepts that shape and its typed inputs; \code{apply_row} proves agreement with the existing row API.}
}

\subtree{
  \title{One boundary transport operation}
  \p{\code{CellGraph.transport} requires equality of complete boundary frames. Identity, composition, inverse and injectivity are checked. \code{castInput} is a restricted wrapper. \code{Operations.substitute_transport} handles row reindexing including the outer cell, and \code{apply_transport} handles equality of complete substitution shapes.}
  \p{\code{Laws.leftUnit}, \code{rightUnit}, \code{insertion} and \code{assoc} now use ordinary equality after transport along the already checked boundary proofs. The vertical-identity equation already has a common boundary. \code{transport_eq_iff_heq} identifies the old and new equation forms given the boundary equality; the mathematical obligation is unchanged. Internal Mathlib comparison adapters retain heterogeneous equalities where needed.}
  \p{\code{SubstitutionExamples.shape_independent} and \code{labels_matter} distinguish incidence from evaluation. \code{cannot_transport_label} rules out transport between different boundaries even when raw labels are heterogeneously equal. The additive model still satisfies all five equations, with the mixed nested value 36 retained.}
}

\subtree{
  \title{Checked vertical extraction and remaining frontier}
  \p{\code{Vertical} derives units, both associativities and interchange from \code{Algebra}. \code{substitute_eq_stack_compose} expresses every nonempty vertical-row substitution as along-row composition followed by stacking. \code{homCategory}, \code{bicategory} and \code{bicategory_strict} construct Mathlib's actual strict 2-category, including coherence. Client associativity and interchange statements are ordinary equalities. Some internal dependent equality remains in these derivations and the Mathlib bridge.}
  \p{\code{NoHorizontal.rowView} exposes the chain of parallel vertical cells forced by empty horizontal edges. \code{rowView_embed} and \code{rowView_length} check its embedded-chain round trip and cell count. \code{FromTwoCategory.operations} defines the reverse substitutions; \code{substitute_vertical} checks their row-fold/stack computation. The reverse algebra laws, comparison round trips and discrete nerve are still open.}
  \p{The free/arity presentation, nerve hypotheses and required labelled diagram-model results remain open. No root or interface freeze is claimed. Reproduce the checked slice with \code{lake build}, \code{python3 scripts/check_foundations.py}, the swap test and forest checks. The inventory records exact declarations and transitive axiom closures under the existing allowlist. Cites: \ref{dec-rt-0030}, \ref{dec-wf-0008}, \ref{dec-wf-0010}.}
}
